      "hints": [
        "Expand $(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)^2=1$ and isolate the fourth-power terms.",
        "Replace $4\\sin^2\\theta\\cos^2\\theta$ by $\\sin^22\\theta$ to reach the identity."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Expand $(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta)^2$ using the binomial square $(A + B)^2 = A^2 + 2AB + B^2$:\n$$(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta)^2 = \\cos^4\\theta + 2\\cos^2\\theta\\sin^2\\theta + \\sin^4\\theta$$",
        "Apply the Pythagorean identity $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1$ to the left-hand side:\n$$1^2 = 1 = \\cos^4\\theta + \\sin^4\\theta + 2(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta)^2$$",
        "Use the double-angle identity $\\sin 2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta \\implies \\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin 2\\theta$:\n$$2(\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta)^2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin 2\\theta\\right)^2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\sin^2 2\\theta\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin^2 2\\theta$$",
        "Rearrange to express $\\cos^4\\theta + \\sin^4\\theta$:\n$$\\cos^4\\theta + \\sin^4\\theta \\equiv 1 - \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin^2 2\\theta$$\nThis completes the proof."
      ],
      "checking": {
        "mode": "ai",
        "ai_rubric": {
          "Trigonometric Identities": "Expansion of the square and equates to $1$. Derives the given identity. The final result is $\\cos^4\\theta+\\sin^4\\theta\\equiv1-\\frac12\\sin^22\\theta$."
        },
        "criteria_source": "mark_scheme_structured"
